PLACES=[
N('us-capitol-dc','United States Capitol','Washington','dc','civic','standing',38.88972,-77.00889,'site','open','walk','','1793–1866',
  'The seat of Congress. George Washington laid the cornerstone in 1793 to William Thornton\'s design, and Congress first met in the finished central parts in November 1800. British troops partly burned it in August 1814; it was restored within five years, with Benjamin Latrobe rebuilding the insides within the old brick and sandstone walls. The present cast-iron dome and the big House and Senate wings were designed by Thomas U. Walter in the 1850s, and the dome was finished around 1866.',
  'The cast-iron dome over the 1800 centre and the 1850s wings. Inside, the guided tour goes to the Crypt, the Rotunda under the dome and National Statuary Hall, the old House chamber; the Senate and House galleries are not on it. Visitors may see the historic rooms only on a guided tour.',
  accn='Run by the Architect of the Capitol. Visitors enter by the underground Capitol Visitor Center on the east side and go through security; tours are free, and the Visitor Center recommends booking ahead and arriving without one by 2:30 p.m. Closed Thanksgiving, Christmas, New Year\'s Day and Inauguration Day.',
  hours='Monday to Saturday 8:30 a.m. to 4:30 p.m.; last tour 3:20 p.m.',
  links=[W('United States Capitol'),CVC,['Capitol Visitor Center: visit','https://www.visitthecapitol.gov/plan-visit']],brick='?'),
N('thomas-jefferson-building-dc','Library of Congress, Thomas Jefferson Building','Washington','dc','civic','standing',38.8887,-77.0046,'site','open','walk','','1890–1897',
  'The first building made for the Library of Congress, put up across from the Capitol between 1890 and 1897 after Congress approved it in 1886. Smithmeyer and Pelz designed it, with Edward Pearce Casey finishing the interiors; it opened to the public on November 1, 1897. It was named for Thomas Jefferson, whose books had re-founded the library in 1815, in 1980.',
  'A Beaux-Arts front on First Street SE and one of the richest public interiors in the country, among them the Main Reading Room, which visitors can look into from the first floor at set times. Free timed-entry tickets are needed to go in.',
  accn='Library of Congress, First Street SE. Reserve free timed-entry tickets on the Library\'s site; same-day tickets are released online at 9 a.m. The Library\'s page also gives Thursday hours of 10 a.m. to 8 p.m.; last entry 4:30 p.m. Formal photography needs a permit.',
  hours='Tuesday to Saturday 10 a.m. to 5 p.m. (Thursday to 8 p.m.); closed Sunday and Monday',
  links=[W('Thomas Jefferson Building'),LOC],brick='?'),
N('old-post-office-dc','Old Post Office and Clock Tower','Washington','dc','civic','standing',38.89417,-77.0275,'site','open','walk','','1892–1899',
  'The city\'s main post office and a federal office building, built from 1892 to 1899 in the Richardsonian Romanesque style on Pennsylvania Avenue. It was nearly pulled down for the Federal Triangle in the 1920s and 1930s and again in the 1970s, when an attempt began in 1970; Congress refused the money in 1972 and it was renovated instead, reopening in 1983 with shops and restaurants. It has been a hotel since 2016, as the Waldorf Astoria Washington DC since June 2022.',
  'The 315-foot clock tower holding the Bells of Congress, the third tallest structure in the city leaving out radio towers, over a Romanesque block whose central atrium was given a skylight roof in 1983. Wikipedia says the National Park Service kept control of the tower\'s 270-foot observation deck to show it to visitors; its hours could not be read when this was written.',
  accn='1100 Pennsylvania Avenue NW, next to the Federal Triangle Metro station. The building is a hotel; the clock tower is in the care of the National Park Service. Tower hours not checked.',
  hours='?',links=[W('Old Post Office (Washington, D.C.)')],brick='?'),
N('willard-hotel-dc','Willard Hotel','Washington','dc','hotel','standing',38.89667,-77.03222,'exact','open','walk','','1901',
  'A hotel has stood here since six houses built in 1816 were let as Tennison\'s Hotel; Henry Willard took over in 1847. Abraham Lincoln stayed here before his inauguration in 1861. The present 12-storey Beaux-Arts building by Henry Janeway Hardenbergh opened in 1901, stood empty for years after closing suddenly on July 16, 1968, with plans to pull it down, and was restored with an office wing added in 1986.',
  'The 1901 Beaux-Arts hotel two blocks east of the White House, with its lobby and the long Peacock Alley. A working luxury hotel; interior not checked beyond the article.',
  accn='1401 Pennsylvania Avenue NW, two blocks west of the Metro Center station. A private hotel, open to guests and to visitors of its restaurants and bars; hours not checked.',
  hours='?',links=[W('Willard InterContinental Washington')],brick='?'),
N('mayflower-hotel-dc','Mayflower Hotel','Washington','dc','hotel','standing',38.9044,-77.0398,'site','open','walk','','1922–1925',
  'A large hotel built by the developer Allan E. Walker to designs by Warren and Wetmore with Robert F. Beresford of Washington, on the site of the Convent of the Visitation. It opened on February 18, 1925, at a cost near $11 million, and Walker died three months later. It is the longest continuously run hotel in the Washington area and was called the "Hotel of Presidents".',
  'Indiana limestone on the first three floors and brick with terra cotta trim above. Inside, the long promenade and a cruciform lobby with a coffered skylight, and marble in the public rooms; how much of the 1925 decoration survives was not checked.',
  accn='Connecticut Avenue NW, a block north of the Farragut North Metro station. A private hotel; hours not checked.',
  hours='?',links=[W('Mayflower Hotel')],brick='?'),
N('healy-hall-georgetown','Healy Hall, Georgetown University','Washington','dc','inst','standing',38.90728,-77.07272,'site','view','walk','','1877–1879',
  'The flagship building of Georgetown University, built from 1877 to 1879 for its president Patrick Francis Healy to designs by Smithmeyer and Pelz, who later designed the Library of Congress\'s Jefferson Building. The cost left the college in debt for years, and its auditorium, Gaston Hall, was not finished until 1909. It is a National Historic Landmark.',
  'A long Neo-Romanesque and High Victorian Gothic hall with a tall clock tower. Inside are Riggs Library, one of the few cast-iron libraries left in the country, and Gaston Hall. Interior visiting not checked.',
  accn='Georgetown University main campus. Seen from the campus front lawn; access to the inside not checked.',
  links=[W('Healy Hall')],brick='?'),
N('memorial-continental-hall-dc','Memorial Continental Hall (DAR)','Washington','dc','civic','standing',38.89389,-77.04,'exact','view','walk','','1904–1910',
  'The national headquarters of the Daughters of the American Revolution, designed by Edward Pearce Casey and built from 1904 to 1910. It held the Washington Naval Conference of 1921 to 1922. The oldest of the DAR\'s three buildings on the block, it was made a National Historic Landmark in 1972.',
  'A two-storey building of brick and concrete faced with Vermont marble, with Doric porticos on three sides, a semicircular thirteen-column portico to the south and a drive-through porch on the 17th Street front facing the Ellipse.',
  accn='1776 D Street NW, beside the Ellipse. The DAR\'s visitor page could not be opened when this was written, so whether and when the building opens was not checked: look from the street.',
  links=[W('Memorial Continental Hall')],brick='no'),
N('pan-american-union-building-dc','Pan American Union Building','Washington','dc','civic','standing',38.89288,-77.04026,'exact','view','walk','','1908–1910',
  'The headquarters of the Organization of American States, built largely with Andrew Carnegie\'s money on the site of the Van Ness Mansion. Theodore Roosevelt, Elihu Root and Carnegie laid the cornerstone on May 11, 1908, and it was dedicated in 1910. The first meeting of the International Labour Organization was held here in 1919; it became a National Historic Landmark in 2021.',
  'A white building in the manner of a Greek temple, with broad outside stairs up to bronze gates and a roof of red tiles.',
  accn='17th Street NW between C Street and Constitution Avenue. The Organization of American States\' visitor page could not be opened when this was written; tours not checked. Look from the street.',
  links=[W('Pan American Union Building')],brick='no'),
N('cannon-house-office-building-dc','Cannon House Office Building','Washington','dc','civic','standing',38.88694,-77.00694,'site','view','walk','','1908',
  'The oldest office building of Congress, finished in 1908 to relieve crowding in the Capitol. Thomas Hastings of Carrère and Hastings oversaw it, beside a near twin for the Senate, now the Russell Building. A fifth floor was added by raising the roof in 1913, and a long renovation, wing by wing, began in 2015.',
  'A Beaux-Arts block faced with marble and limestone south of the Capitol. Inside, a rotunda and twin marble stairs lead to the Caucus Room. Visiting the inside not checked.',
  accn='Independence Avenue and First Street SE. A working office building of the House of Representatives; look from the street.',
  links=[W('Cannon House Office Building')],brick='no'),
N('national-savings-and-trust-dc','National Savings and Trust Company Building','Washington','dc','civic','standing',38.89922,-77.03333,'exact','view','walk','','1888',
  'A bank building by James H. Windrim, built in 1888 at New York Avenue and 15th Street. It was also called the National Safe Deposit Company. The bank passed through Crestar and SunTrust, and Wikipedia last gives it as a Truist branch.',
  'A Queen Anne building of red brick, rich with copper and terra cotta detail.',
  accn='New York Avenue and 15th Street NW. A bank building; look from the street.',
  links=[W('National Savings and Trust Company')],brick='yes'),
N('old-patent-office-dc','Old Patent Office Building','Washington','dc','civic','standing',38.89781,-77.023,'site','open','walk','','1836–1867',
  'A Greek Revival building that fills a city block, begun in 1836 to Robert Mills\'s design and carried on by Thomas U. Walter; Wikipedia gives both 1865 and 1867 for its completion; Mills vaulted the rooms in masonry to keep fire out. In 1877 a fire in the west wing destroyed some 87,000 patent models, and Adolf Cluss rebuilt it. The Patent Office left in 1932, and in 1968 the building opened as two Smithsonian museums, the American Art Museum and the National Portrait Gallery.',
  'The Greek Revival block between F and G Streets. Inside, the Smithsonian American Art Museum and the National Portrait Gallery; the American Art Museum gives the hours below.',
  accn='Between F and G and 7th and 9th Streets NW. Free; closed December 25, as the American Art Museum\'s page says.',
  hours='Daily 11:30 a.m. to 7 p.m.',links=[W('Old Patent Office Building'),SAAM],brick='?'),
N('renwick-gallery-dc','Renwick Gallery','Washington','dc','civic','standing',38.89911,-77.03906,'exact','open','walk','','1859–1874',
  'Built as the city\'s first art museum, for William Wilson Corcoran\'s collection, to James Renwick Jr.\'s design after the Louvre\'s Tuileries wing. The Army seized it in 1861 as a warehouse, and General Montgomery Meigs had his headquarters here in 1864; it was finished in 1874. The Smithsonian took it over in 1965 and reopened it in 1972 for American craft; after a renovation it reopened in 2015.',
  'The 1859 gallery on Pennsylvania Avenue, modelled on the Louvre\'s Tuileries wing, and inside the Grand Salon, renovated as an event space. A branch of the Smithsonian American Art Museum.',
  accn='1661 Pennsylvania Avenue NW at 17th Street. Free; closed December 25. Main entrance by stairs on Pennsylvania Avenue, ramp at the 17th Street entrance. The gallery\'s page says on-street parking is limited and metered, with paid garages nearby.',
  hours='Daily 10 a.m. to 5:30 p.m.',links=[W('Renwick Gallery'),['Smithsonian American Art Museum: Renwick Gallery','https://americanart.si.edu/visit/renwick']],brick='?'),
N('eisenhower-executive-office-building-dc','Eisenhower Executive Office Building (State, War, and Navy Building)','Washington','dc','civic','standing',38.89757,-77.03915,'site','view','walk','','1871–1888',
  'Built from 1871 to 1888 for the State, War and Navy departments, to Alfred B. Mullett\'s design in the French Second Empire style, which was scorned at the time beside the city\'s classical buildings. Its granite was cut on Vinalhaven, Maine, and it cost just over $10 million. It now holds offices of the White House staff and was renamed for Dwight D. Eisenhower in 1999.',
  'A huge granite building in the French Second Empire style beside the White House. Inside are cast-iron stairs and decoration by Richard von Ezdorf; restored offices, not open to the public.',
  accn='17th Street NW at Pennsylvania Avenue, part of the White House grounds. Look from the street.',
  links=[W('Eisenhower Executive Office Building')],brick='no'),
N('house-of-the-temple-dc','House of the Temple','Washington','dc','civic','standing',38.9138,-77.0359,'exact','open','walk','','1911–1915',
  'The headquarters of the Scottish Rite of Freemasonry, Southern Jurisdiction, designed by John Russell Pope after the Mausoleum at Halicarnassus and finished in 1915. In the 1920s a panel of architects named it one of the three best public buildings in the country. Its library opened in 1870 as the first public library in Washington.',
  'A temple on 16th Street modelled on the Mausoleum at Halicarnassus. The Scottish Rite gives free guided tours and says the building has been open for them since 1915.',
  accn='1733 16th Street NW, Dupont Circle. Free guided tours; groups of ten or more should email ahead. Closed some holidays; the Scottish Rite\'s page lists them. The library is open Monday to Thursday, 9 a.m. to 4:30 p.m.',
  hours='Tours Monday to Thursday at 10 and 11 a.m., 12:30, 2 and 3:30 p.m.',
  links=[W('House of the Temple'),['Scottish Rite: Hours and tours','https://scottishrite.org/about/house-of-the-temple/tours/']],brick='no'),
N('perry-belmont-house-dc','Perry Belmont House (Order of the Eastern Star)','Washington','dc','landmark','standing',38.91222,-77.04167,'exact','ask','walk','','1907–1909',
  'A Louis XVI Beaux-Arts mansion designed by the French architect Ernest-Paul Sanson for the politician Perry Belmont, built from 1907 to 1909 under Horace Trumbauer. Belmont sold it in 1935 to the General Grand Chapter of the Order of the Eastern Star, whose international headquarters it still is.',
  'A one-storey stone pavilion with Ionic pilasters over a rusticated basement, and a mansard attic hidden behind a balustrade with urns. Wikipedia says most of the inside is kept as in Belmont\'s day, with French ironwork, German wood and Italian marble.',
  accn='1618 New Hampshire Avenue NW, Dupont Circle. Wikipedia says public tours can be made by appointment; the Order\'s own page was not read. Otherwise look from the street.',
  links=[W('Perry Belmont House')],brick='no'),
N('m-street-high-school-dc','M Street High School','Washington','dc','civic','standing',38.9054,-77.0134,'exact','view','walk','','1890–1891',
  'Built in 1890 and 1891 for the Washington High School for Colored Youth, founded in 1870 and one of the first high schools for Black students in the country; its graduates went on to Harvard, Yale and Brown. Thomas Entwistle designed it in the Romanesque Revival style. Saved from sale to developers in the 1980s, it was listed on the National Register in 1986 and has been a community service center since 1998.',
  'The Romanesque Revival school building on M Street. Interior not checked.',
  accn='M Street NW. A community service center; look from the street.',
  links=[W('M Street High School')],brick='?'),
N('city-post-office-national-postal-museum-dc','City Post Office (National Postal Museum)','Washington','dc','civic','standing',38.89806,-77.00833,'site','open','walk','','1914',
  'The city\'s main post office from 1914 until 1986, designed by Graham and Burnham, the firm Ernest Graham led after Daniel Burnham\'s death in 1912. Since 1993 it has held the Smithsonian\'s National Postal Museum, with mail planes, trains and vehicles in its atrium.',
  'The 1914 post office across the street from Union Station. Inside, the museum\'s atrium and stamp galleries; a Postal Service window still sells stamps.',
  accn='Across the street from Union Station; the station has a four-level paid garage. The museum\'s page refused to open when this was written: hours not checked.',
  hours='?',links=[W('National Postal Museum')],brick='?'),
N('union-station-dc','Washington Union Station','Washington','dc','rail','standing',38.89731,-77.00626,'site','open','walk','','1903–1908',
  'One of the country\'s first great union railroad terminals, planned under Daniel Burnham and drawn mostly by William Pierce Anderson of his firm. It opened in 1907 and the main building was done in 1908; in wartime up to 200,000 people a day passed through. Restored and given a shopping mall in 1988, it is Amtrak\'s second-busiest station, and Amtrak took it over in 2024 for another renovation.',
  'The Burnham station front and its Main Hall, still a working station with Amtrak, commuter trains and the Metro.',
  accn='Union Station, Capitol Hill. The station says it is closed to the public daily from 11 p.m. to 5 a.m., when only ticketed passengers may enter; shop hours vary. Four-level paid garage at the back.',
  hours='Daily 5 a.m. to 11 p.m. (ticketed passengers at all hours)',
  links=[W('Washington Union Station'),['Union Station: hours','https://www.unionstationdc.com/hours/']],brick='no'),
]
